where the index \( i \) matches the chosen nontrivial operator in \( p \). This asymmetry in \( \beta^T \beta \) furnishes a swap-intolerant nonlocality as \( \mathcal{M} \) is given by the sum of the largest two eigenvalues of the above matrix. Let \( (\beta_1, \beta_2, \beta_3) \) be the vector entries of \( \beta \) for a given semi-trivial perp state \( \rho \), and WLOG let \( \beta_3 \) be the smallest of the three. Then the following conditions give a SIN state:

\[
\begin{aligned}
\beta_1^2 + \beta_2^2 + \beta_3^2 &> 1 \\
\beta_1^2 + \beta_2^2 &\leq 1 \\
\beta_3^2 &\leq \min(\beta_1^2, \beta_2^2)
\end{aligned}
\tag{9}
\]

These conditions give \( \mathcal{M} > 1 \) for \( p = I \otimes i \) (left-identity) but not \( p = i \otimes I \) (right-identity). The first describes the region outside the unit sphere in \( S \), the second describes the interior of the unit cylinder along the \( \beta_3 \) axis, and the third gives the exterior of the cone along the \( \beta_3 \) axis with endpoint at the origin. The intersection of these regions is nonempty in \( S \). We need to place our state point \( E \) in this intersection and find suitable coordinates \( \tau_i \) and \( \tau_p \) to describe our state.

